  "worked_solution": "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={-1,3}\\times [2,1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $-1$ or $3$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-2,1].$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {-1,3},\\,y\\in [-2,1]}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}\u2282S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-2,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-2,1]}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/57cb74832a9766c60819bdcc0c88290c4a05556b673965973e161895de2f530a.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}\u2282S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $3$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-2,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(3,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-2,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/9f3d0beca2ed9eb30eb6456b67d5f17002726e9b9de6e8ed3c79d72f43bcf3ae.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the two line segments shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040430/assets/d19ecceb011b54492d3ea9f0d262de1ba96e21c3423ed8b083d44551a6420db9.png)",
